<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sima Yi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">179–251</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sima Yi was a general and statesman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>. He defended Wei against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>'s northern campaigns, destroyed the Gongsun regime in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> in 238, and in 249 seized control of the court in a coup against Cao Shuang, laying the foundations for his grandson's Jin dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sima-yi.jpg" alt="Sima Yi" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ming Zhu Tianran · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%AD%8F%C2%B7%E5%A4%AA%E5%82%85_%E5%8F%B8%E9%A6%AC%E6%87%BF.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sima Yi, courtesy name Zhongda, came from Xiaojing in Wen County, Henei Commandery, and traced his ancestry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-ang">Sima Ang</a>, King of Yin, who briefly ruled one of the Eighteen Kingdoms in the transition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> to the Western Han. He showed intelligence and ambition young and knew the Confucian classics well. Before he was about 19, the commandery administrator Yang Jun, known for spotting talent, called him an extraordinary talent, and Cui Yan, a friend of his elder brother Sima Lang, said Sima Lang could not compare with him. He formally began his political career in 208 under the Imperial Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 215 Sima Yi accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> against Zhang Lu at Yangping and urged him to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> in Yi Province, but Cao Cao declined. He also warned that Hu Xiu and Fu Fang were unfit to guard the border at Jing Province, and in 219, at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fancheng">Fancheng</a>, both defected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>. When Cao Cao died in March 220 he supervised the funeral arrangements.</p><p>Under Cao Rui he defeated Zhuge Jin's attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a>, and in July 227 he was sent to garrison Wan and given the military affairs of Jing and Yu provinces. In March 230 he was promoted to General-in-Chief. He is best known for defending Wei against a series of invasions by Shu between 231 and 234, and in 238 he led an army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-yuan">Gongsun Yuan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. Sun Quan wrote that Sima Yi used strategy like a god.</p><p>In 239 he became a regent for the young Cao Fang alongside Cao Shuang. Their relations soured over Cao Shuang's corruption, extravagance and attempts to curtail Sima Yi's influence, and in February 249, after careful preparation, Sima Yi ousted Cao Shuang in a coup and had him and his associates executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Afterwards Sima Yi was the main authority in Wei. In June 251 he faced Wang Ling's rebellion, which he dealt with swiftly. He died on 7 September 251 at the age of 71 or 72 and was succeeded by his eldest son Sima Shi. For the rest of Wei's history power passed increasingly to the Sima clan, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin">Sima Yan</a> founded the Jin dynasty in February 266, honouring him as Emperor Xuan of Jin with the temple name Gaozu.</p><p>He was the last common ancestor of all Jin emperors: the Western Jin descended from his son Sima Zhao, the Eastern Jin from his son Sima Zhou. In 249 Wang Guang said that Sima Yi gave his assignments to the worthy and capable and had left nothing that Cao Shuang did wrong uncorrected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sima Yi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 249 <b>Sima Yi</b> seized power in the Gaoping Tombs incident, and the Sima family ruled through puppet emperors.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>In 238 Cao Wei sent <b>Sima Yi</b>, who besieged Xiangping, captured and executed Gongsun Yuan and massacred thousands of men.</blockquote><a href="/state/gongsun-yan">Gongsun Yan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wen County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.94,113.07&amp;year=225" aria-label="Wen County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sima Yi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4516 4563h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4516 4563h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.94,113.07&amp;year=225">What was here in 225 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
